A host's agent starts runs of its own that pi never began: as a conversation
reopens, it carries on the turn a restart cut off, and asks again a request
the restart cut off. pi prepares only the runs it starts from a message, so
these went out on the session's base prompt options. Every section an
extension adds in before_agent_start (its resident block, standing context,
the index of tools it can load) was dropped from the request and recorded
as dropped, then put back at the next prompt, rewriting the provider's
cache twice. The session also read idle through the run, so extensions'
steers were refused, and no agent_settled ever said it ended.

runForHost runs the host's work as one of pi's runs with no starting
message: busy from its start, before_agent_start fired with a "continue"
trigger, its prompt options kept for every turn of the run, and pi's own
settle at its end. A message a handler returns waits for the next prompt,
as a nextTurn message does, so none lands between a tool call and its
result. A handler's edit of selectedTools goes into the live tool loadout,
as a prompt's run applies it, so the model is offered what the handler
chose, and restored tools not yet registered are dropped, as a prompt's
run drops them. The system message that brings the prompt's sections up
to date is handed to the work, for the host to record before the run's
first request: after a restart that changed the sections, as an upgrade
does, that request would otherwise go out on the old ones. A runtime
without this hands the work undefined. A run that fails to start keeps
what it held for the next turn or run, since the transcript it would carry
on may still owe a tool result. hostRuns marks a runtime that has it.
Nothing in pi calls it, so a pi no host drives behaves as before.

Its hunk sits on _startRun's own ending, so a pi whose run lifecycle moved
fails to apply here rather than in a host. Apply only to the host's private
pi build; ordinary interactive pi stays unchanged.

